  VivaQuestion(id: 'gen-a2', topicId: 'genomics', stage: 3, question: 'A Genomic Estimated Breeding Value (GEBV) is calculated by:', options: ['Averaging the parents\' phenotypes', 'Summing marker effects estimated from a genome-wide prediction model', 'Counting the number of favorable alleles only at one locus', 'Measuring a single field trial\'s yield'], correctIndex: 1, followUpCorrect: 'Correct. Why can GEBVs be calculated for an individual before it has ever been phenotyped in the field?', followUpIncorrect: 'GEBVs come from a statistical model applied across THOUSANDS of markers genome-wide, not from phenotype averaging or a single locus.'),
  VivaQuestion(id: 'gen-a3', topicId: 'genomics', stage: 3, question: 'Ridge regression BLUP and Bayesian methods used in genomic selection primarily differ in:', options: ['Whether they use DNA markers at all', 'The assumed distribution of marker effect sizes', 'Whether phenotypic data is required', 'The crop species they can be applied to'], correctIndex: 1, followUpCorrect: 'Right. Which method tends to perform better when a trait is controlled by a few large-effect QTL rather than purely polygenic variation?', followUpIncorrect: 'Both use markers and phenotypes — the real difference is a statistical assumption about how large or small most marker effects are expected to be.'),
